<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agilulf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 616</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Agilulf was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from 590 to 616. Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, he became king by marrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodelinda">Theodelinda</a>, widow of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/authari">Authari</a>, and consolidated Lombard rule, fighting the Byzantine exarchate of Ravenna and making peace with Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a>. Under Theodelinda's influence he allowed their son Adaloald to be baptised a Catholic, a step towards the conversion of the Lombards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agilulf.jpg" alt="Agilulf" width="245" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michel Wolgemut, Wilhelm Pleydenwurff (Text: Hartmann Schedel) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuremberg_chronicles_f_150r_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Agilulf was born around 555 and was also known as the Thuringian. He belonged to the Anawas clan and was of Thuringian origin, a relative of his predecessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/authari">Authari</a>. He is sometimes called dux Turingorum de Taurinis, which suggests he was a prominent leader among the Thuringians who had joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> after their kingdom fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in 531.</p><p>He was chosen as king on the advice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodelinda">Theodelinda</a>, the Christian queen and widow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/authari">Authari</a>, and he later married her. He took the royal title in November 590, and his formal investiture, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> warriors raised him on a shield, took place at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in May 591.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Agilulf was baptised to please his wife, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> followed, at first adopting the Arian rather than the Catholic faith. In 603, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodelinda">Theodelinda</a>'s influence, he converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and had his son Adaloald baptised in the same rite. Together they built and endowed the Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monza">Monza</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-crown-of-lombardy">Iron Crown of Lombardy</a> is still kept. His own crown, now lost, was kept there too and was inscribed rex totius Italiae, king of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>Hostilities with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> ceased after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guntram">Guntram</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, in 592, because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> then fell into civil war. In 598 Agilulf made a truce with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">Papacy</a>, ending three decades of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> incursions into the Ducatus Romanus, and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutri">Sutri</a>, Perugia and other Umbrian cities from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exarchate-of-ravenna">Exarchate of Ravenna</a>. He also made a truce that year with the Byzantine emperor Maurice, with the help of Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a>, but in 599 the Exarch Callinicus broke it by abducting Agilulf's daughter. In 602 the Lombards took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> and then Mantua, and in 605 the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocas">Phocas</a> recognised Agilulf, agreed to pay tribute and ceded Orvieto and other towns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 607 Witteric, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, formed an alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theuderic-ii">Theuderic II of Burgundy</a> that included Theudebert II of Austrasia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chlothar-ii">Clotaire II</a> of Neustria and Agilulf, but it appears to have achieved little. In 610 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a> and killed its duke, Gisulf. The last years of the reign were largely peaceful, and Agilulf died in April 616 after ruling for over a quarter of a century. His son Adaloald succeeded him, still an adolescent. His daughter Gundeberga later married Arioald, a future king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Agilulf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the start the duchy stood under Frankish overlordship, yet the Agilolfings had wide connections: Garibald's daughter Theodelinda married the Lombard king Authari in 589 and later <b>Agilulf</b>, and became a leading figure in Lombard Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=606" aria-label="Milan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4768 560 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Agilulf"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5218 830h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5218 830h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=45.47,9.19&amp;year=606">What was here in 606 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
